{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788209636,"nonce":"lhzI6UoVm8EdwN7yt3cqp2WRQyo=","message":"PAIRS WITH the new sampling issue 2d9a1da — read it before writing this spec.\n\nEvan and the orchestrator, 2026-08-31. Two reasons they should be designed\ntogether rather than one after the other:\n\n1. **This spec's post-mortem half is that spec's first consumer.** A post-mortem\n   with only the journal can say \"we died to a raid\". With samples it can say\n   \"we died to a raid at 14 food-days and 0.3 weapons per colonist\". That is the\n   difference between a lesson that oscillates — died to raids, over-arm, starve,\n   over-farm, die to raids — and one that converges, because it records the\n   TRADEOFF and its cost rather than the cause alone. Evan's own framing of the\n   goal is a colony that rebalances over about three games; that is a damping\n   problem, and damping needs numbers.\n\n2. **\"What is worth remembering\" and \"what is worth looking at each morning\" are\n   the same question one tick apart.** Session 6 recorded that 2.1 (the digest)\n   was specced and built before this issue existed, so the CODE decided what\n   mattered before Evan did. Writing the morning checklist and the sample schema\n   independently would repeat that mistake exactly — you would get a checklist\n   reading values nobody archived, and an archive full of values nobody checks.\n\nEvan's steer on WHAT matters, in his words, 2026-08-31: the game's alert system\nis largely good enough, \"with the exception of stocking up on food, medicine, and\nespecially weapons since raids will knock you out.\" Weapons is the sharpest of\nthe three, because no vanilla alert covers armament at all — six colonists and\ntwo guns and the game never says a word. Those three belong in the checklist for\nthe same reason they belong in the sample row: alerts are lagging by\nconstruction, and those are the ones with no alert at all.","files":null}]}